course: dictionary
lesson: 10182
title: Tightrope: real and in phrases
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A tightrope is a rope or wire stretched tight high above the ground, and an acrobat walks along it. The name tells you what it is: a rope pulled tight.
explain: «A tightrope walker carries a long pole because it helps her keep her centre of gravity over the rope.» The pole helps her balance, so she does not fall. «A tightrope walker's long pole is slow to turn, which gives the walker time to correct wobbles.» Small wobbles can be fixed in time.
explain: «To walk a tightrope» is a phrase. It means being in a difficult situation where you must be very careful, because one small mistake could cause trouble. Check it by putting your meaning into the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: A tightrope is a rope or wire stretched tight high above the ground.
fact: An acrobat is a performer who does balancing and jumping tricks.
fact: A tightrope walker often carries a long pole to help keep balance.
fact: «To walk a tightrope» means to be in a difficult situation where you must be very careful.
fact: Centre of gravity is the point where an object's weight is balanced; keeping it over the rope helps a walker stay up.
exercise: What does an acrobat do on a tightrope? | Digs a hole under it | Sells tickets for it | *Walks along it
exercise: Nina has to walk a tightrope between her two arguing friends. What does that mean? | She has decided to stop talking | *She must be very careful to keep both sides happy | She is cheering for both of them | She is walking on a real rope
exercise: Why does a tightrope walker carry a long pole? | It is used to wave to the crowd | It stops the rope from breaking | It is a good stick to lean on | *It helps her keep her centre of gravity over the rope
test: In «on a tightrope», what kind of situation is it? | A calm, easy place to rest | *A difficult one where one small mistake could cause trouble | A place high in the trees | A long, empty road
test: What does the long pole help the walker keep? | Her hat on | A pet bird | Her lunch | *Her balance
exam: Which sentence uses «walk a tightrope» correctly? | The tightrope was made of soft wool | I walked my dog to the shop | *Marta has to walk a tightrope between her two strict teachers' rules | The tightrope is in the box
